    {
      "id": "time-wait",
      "tab": "Scenario 2",
      "short": "TIME_WAIT runs out the ports",
      "view": "ports",
      "q": "A worker opens a new connection for every call to one destination. Why does it start failing with \"Cannot assign requested address\" when the server is fine?",
      "frames": [
        {
          "clock": "1 s",
          "badge": "each closed connection holds its port for 60 s",
          "t": 1,
          "term": "for item in batch:\n    requests.get(url)  # new connection; client closes it",
          "h": "One connection's life",
          "b": "A connection is identified by source IP, source port, destination IP and destination port. Here only the client's source port can change. Each call takes a free port, does its request, and the client closes. The side that closes first keeps its socket in TIME_WAIT, here for 60 s, and the port can't be reused for this destination until then."
        },
        {
          "clock": "1 s",
          "badge": "why: stray segments, lost final ACK",
          "t": 1,
          "why": true,
          "h": "Why TIME_WAIT exists",
          "b": "A segment from the old connection might still be delayed in the network. If a new connection reused the same four values immediately, that stray segment could be accepted into it. TIME_WAIT also lets the closer resend its final ACK if that was lost. The spec says 2 × MSL; Linux uses a fixed 60 s."
        },
        {
          "clock": "10 s",
          "badge": "≈ 10,000 ports in TIME_WAIT",
          "t": 10,
          "h": "The grid fills",
          "b": "At 1,000 connections a second, each one parks a port for 60 s. After 10 s, about 10,000 of the ~28,000 ports are in TIME_WAIT."
        },
        {
          "clock": "28 s → 120 s",
          "badge": "out of ports: Errno 99 · ≈ 470/s on average",
          "bad": true,
          "t": 120,
          "term": "OSError: [Errno 99] Cannot assign requested address",
          "h": "Out of ports",
          "b": "At about 28 s every port is taken and <code>connect()</code> fails; <code>requests</code> raises a <code>ConnectionError</code>. New connections now succeed only as ports leave TIME_WAIT, in bursts, averaging 28,232 ÷ 60 ≈ 470 a second. The server is idle the whole time."
        },
        {
          "clock": "fix",
          "badge": "one Session: a handful of ports, no TIME_WAIT",
          "t": 120,
          "fix": true,
          "term": "s = requests.Session()\nfor item in batch:\n    s.get(url)  # reuses the same connection",
          "h": "Fix: keep-alive",
          "b": "One <code>requests.Session</code> (or a small pool) carries all 1,000 requests a second on a few connections. Nothing closes, so nothing enters TIME_WAIT. If connections really must be short-lived: more destination IPs or ports, a wider port range, or <code>tcp_tw_reuse=1</code> for outgoing connections (Linux default 2 allows it for loopback only). Never <code>tcp_tw_recycle</code>, removed in Linux 4.12."
        }
      ],
      "take": "TIME_WAIT sits on whichever side closes first. On a client talking to one destination, it uses up the only value in the four-tuple that can change.",
      "gen": "Proxies and sidecars opening a connection per request, NAT gateways with many clients behind one IP, load tests run without keep-alive."
    },
